    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "Directory conventions — what an instance declares it scans",
      "prov:value": "**It is the *harness's* schema, not folio-assistant's.** `agentic-harness` is\nthe layer that defines Roles, Skills, Tools and these conventions, and every\nother instance inherits from it — so an instance need not be a folio-assistant\nto carry one. A Tool repo or a Test repo carries the same declaration. Issue\n[#223](https://github.com/litlfred/folio-assistant/issues/223), Phase 0.3.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#223"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Why directories rather than a content type",
      "prov:value": "## Node Kinds are declared: `nodeSchemas` (owner, 2026-09-29)",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-29"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-29"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The graph typologies",
      "prov:value": "> ### `kg` was renamed to `cat-harness` (2026-09-19)\n>\n> Every other harness concept is named for the **layer that defines it** — the\n> `CatHarness` declaration, the `cat-harness` instance. `kg` named what the\n> graph HOLDS instead, and was the odd one out.\n>\n> The declaration FILE was a third example until the 2026-09-21 split made it\n> `<name>.json`. It is named for the **instance** now, so it argues nothing\n> here either way.\n>\n> **The old spelling still reads, and that is load-bearing rather than\n> politeness.** This document says overrides match on the entry's `id`, not\n> its `path`, because \"matching on path makes two knowledge graphs out of one\n> relocation\". The same property makes renaming a kind a CROSS-INSTANCE\n> breaking change: a downstream instance declaring `graphs: [\"kg\"]` would\n> resolve nothing, and would do so **silently** — scanning, finding nothing,\n> reporting a clean run. That is the `dh4f` defect delivered to somebody\n> else's repository.\n>\n> So `GRAPH_TYPOLOGY_ALIASES` in `schemas/kg/cat-harness.ts` maps `kg` →\n> `cat-harness`, resolved once at the registry boundary and nowhere else.\n> `resolveGraphTypology` reports WHICH spelling was used, so a caller can warn —\n> an alias that resolves silently is an alias nobody ever removes.\n>\n> **A directory `id` of `kg` is untouched.** An id is the instance's own\n> handle and must survive a rename of anything else; this repository's own\n> declaration moved to `id: \"cat-harness\"` because it chose to, not because\n> it had to, and moved on to `id: \"skills\"` on 2026-09-23. A Subgraph id is\n> its name inside its Harness, and the Harness name qualifies it\n> (`cat-harness.skills`). `RENAMED_DIRECTORY_IDS` reads the old id as the new\n> one, so an older declaration still overrides the entry it meant.\n>\n> **The JSON-LD projection canonicalises.** `toJsonLd` emits the type IRI, not\n> the spelling, so reading a projection back yields `cat-harness` whatever was\n> written. Project-and-read-back is therefore a migration path for an old\n> declaration rather than a way to lose information.\n>\n> Not renamed, deliberately: `kg:audit`, `kg-export`, `kg-qa` sidecars and the\n> `kg` QA family. Those are command and file-format names, not the graph typology,\n> and churning them would touch hundreds of references for no gain in what a\n> declaration MEANS.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-19",
        "2026-09-21",
        "2026-09-23"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-19",
        "2026-09-21",
        "2026-09-23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The graph typologies",
      "prov:value": "**The table of every kind is a published page, not this skill.** Each row is a\nkind's `description`, and the rows span every instance in the checkout:\nThe FHIR IG layer, the science layer and the DAK layer each declare kinds of their own.\nSo the table is built at publish, by `bun run cat kind:table`, at\n`<base>/cat-harness/kind-table/` (`docs/cat-harness/kind-table.md`, declared in\ncat-harness's `builtAtPublish`). When it lived in this skill, the skill went stale\nat every re-pin that brought a layer's new kind (owner, 2026-10-14: \"1\"). To\nchange a row, edit the kind.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-14"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-5",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The graph typologies",
      "prov:value": "> ### `test/` is the one test tree — resolved 2026-09-19\n>\n> This instance declares `qa` at **`test/results/`** and `health` at\n> **`test/health/results/`**, and everything a test runner reads is under\n> `test/`: the Playwright `*.e2e.ts` specs, their `support/` fixtures, and the\n> health sweep's own `*.test.ts`.\n>\n> **It was two trees for a day.** There was never a principle behind the `s`:\n> `test/` grew around the QA results tree, `tests/` around the Playwright specs\n> that `playwright.config.ts` reached with `testDir: './tests'`, and the health\n> sweep landed under `tests/health/` in #395 because the owner asked for it\n> there. That PR recorded the inconsistency here rather than resolving it, on\n> the grounds that relocating a declared directory as a side effect of adding a\n> graph typology is how a directory quietly goes missing. The owner settled it the\n> same day — bean `auap`, PR #400 — and it moved as its own change, with its\n> own review.\n>\n> **Why `test/` won, and it is the rule for the next one of these.** An id in a\n> declaration is the expensive thing to move: `GRAPH_TYPOLOGY_ALIASES` exists\n> because renaming one is a cross-instance breaking change, and a downstream\n> instance overriding a path that is no longer declared scans nothing and\n> reports a clean run over it — the `dh4f` defect. `test/` was the side a\n> declaration pointed at; `tests/` was the side reached by one line of one\n> config file. **Move the cheap side.** The `health` entry's `path` changed\n> and its **`id` did not**, which is what makes the move survivable for an\n> inheriting instance: `resolveDirectories` matches an override on the entry's\n> id, never on its path.\n>\n> **Verify a move like this positively, never on a green test run.** The\n> failure mode is a consumer that now scans nothing and reports clean, which\n> looks identical to success from inside. Three constants here composed their\n> path from segments — `join(\"tests\", \"health\", \"results\")` in\n> `schemas/sdlc/health-report.ts`, and the scan-root lists in\n> `scripts/repo-partition.ts` and `scripts/tests/site-dir-single-answer.test.ts`\n> — so a corpus grep for the moved path did not find any of them, and all three\n> fail **silently** when stale. What caught them was asking each tool to name\n> its subjects: `bun run cat harness:dirs:check` listing `test/health/results` as\n> `ok` among 11 declared, `bun run cat health:list` naming all five checks, and\n> Playwright reporting a non-zero spec count.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean auap",
        "#395",
        "#400",
        "2026-09-19"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-19"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-6",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The graph typologies",
      "prov:value": "> **A rendered translation is not its own graph typology — the FILE declares its\n> language.** `docs/fr/index.md` carries `lang: fr` and\n> `translation_source: index.md` in its own front matter, so it is the same\n> kind of thing as the page it translates: renderable content, differing by a\n> field. `pipeline/translation-index.ts` discovers the locale subtrees\n> by reading those fields and **never** matches a directory name against a\n> list of language subtags — that inference is wrong in both directions,\n> silently hiding a `no/` chapter (Norwegian, or the English word) and\n> silently showing a `pt-BR/` or `translated-fr/` one.\n>\n> A first draft of PR #351 did give it a kind, with a `locale` field and one\n> declaration per locale subtree: ten entries for five locales across two\n> subtrees, restating what all ten files already said, growing as\n> O(locales x subtrees). The owner's framing is what settles it —\n> *\"narrative/audio/visual content with text should be translatable. its not\n> so much the node schema itself but its content (e.g. markdown, bpmn) should\n> be translatable\"*: translatability is a property of a **format within a\n> content type**, which the owning instance declares under\n> `contentTranslations` (collected by `schemas/library/translation-tools.ts`) and\n> `isTranslatable` already answers, not a property of a directory.\n>\n> `.po` catalogues are the genuine exception, and that is what\n> `translation-sources` is for: they are not content in any language, so no\n> file inside them can declare one.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#351"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-7",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "The graph typologies",
      "prov:value": "> **`uploads` and `library` are two kinds, not one, and the split is\n> load-bearing.** They are the two stages of the document-ingestion pipeline,\n> and the corpus-grep checklist searches `library/` **only** — so a source\n> still sitting in `uploads/` does not merely go unread, it makes a clean grep\n> read as *\"nobody has done this\"* while the file is on disk. An un-ingested\n> source is worse than an absent one, because it produces false confidence\n> rather than a gap. One `sources` kind covering both would erase exactly the\n> distinction the pair exists to state.\n>\n> **They are also the reason `materialiseDirectories` exists.** `AGENTS.md`\n> says *\"declare only what exists — a declared-but-absent directory is the bean\n> `dh4f` defect, where a consumer scans nothing and reports a clean run over\n> it\"*. Absent and empty are indistinguishable to a consumer, so declaring a\n> directory without creating it converts a real gap into a false pass.\n> `bun run cat harness:dirs` creates every directory an instance declares or\n> inherits; the session-start sweep and `init-folio` both call it, so a\n> declaration is never left without the directory it names."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-8",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The graph typologies",
      "prov:value": "> **`todos` is not a second work plan, and the distinction is the one\n> `AGENTS.md` already draws.** `beans/` is the AGENT work plan and no second\n> store may be stood up beside it. `todos/` is the other thing that document\n> carves out — *\"the content-review feedback workflow … a separate domain\n> feature, not the agent work-plan\"* — and it is **content**: a todo records\n> that a **person** has something outstanding, and it is owned and authored by\n> the folio.\n>\n> **A todo carries the four coordinates of the role model**, because they are\n> already declared and inventing a fifth vocabulary for them would be the drift\n> this file exists to prevent. `AGENTS.md`: *an actor performs a task in a\n> process as a role.* A todo is that sentence left unfinished, so it is tagged\n> by `roles`, `processes`, `tasks` and `identities` (`schemas/sdlc/todo.ts`).\n>\n> Two things about those tags are load-bearing rather than incidental:\n>\n> - **A task reference carries its process.** A BPMN activity id is unique only\n>   *within* its process, so a task tag is the pair `{ process, task }` and never\n>   a bare string. Same lesson as the subprocess interpreter: a step id without\n>   its phase is not an address.\n> - **An identity is provider-qualified, and its link to an actor is optional.**\n>   `litlfred` is not an identity; `github:litlfred` is. The link to a declared\n>   actor is absent whenever the person is not in `cat-harness/scenarios/actors/` — and\n>   somebody who comments on a pull request is a real person with a real\n>   outstanding item whether or not the registry has heard of them. Absent means\n>   **not linked**, a third state; never anonymous, and never defaulted.\n>\n> `resolveTodoTags` reports each tag as `resolved`, `dangling` or\n> **`not-checked`**, and the third is the one that matters: a checkout with no\n> `skills/` must report every role tag as unchecked, never as dangling. A wall\n> of false findings is how a check gets switched off.\n>\n> **`todos/` was previously a machine-queue directory, and that is retired, not\n> revived.** Four audit scripts defaulted their bulk JSON into `todos/*.json`\n> (bean `bfyw`); all four now write under `build/`, and\n> `scripts/tests/audit-output-paths.test.ts` pins that none of them regresses.\n> That guard forbids machine output landing there. It does not forbid the\n> declared content graph described here, and the two must not be confused:\n> bulk machine-generated queues stay bulk JSON under `build/`, exactly as\n> before.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean bfyw"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-9",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The graph typologies",
      "prov:value": "> **`bean-defs` and `workflow-state` are the distinction `beans` swallowed.**\n> Collapsing `workplan` + `process-state` into one `beans` kind was right about\n> the *mechanism* — one directory, one declaration — but the underlying\n> difference is real and came back as nodes: WHAT IS BEING WORKED ON carries\n> judgement and is edited by hand; WHERE A RUNNING PROCESS GOT TO is a token the\n> interpreter owns. A consumer asking for the work plan must still not be handed\n> BPMN instance state."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-10",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The graph typologies",
      "prov:value": "**One `beans` kind, and the distinction it used to carry moved inward.** An\nearlier version declared **two** directories here — `workplan` at `beans/` and\n`process-state` at `beans/workflow/` — on the reasoning that WHAT IS BEING\nWORKED ON and WHERE A RUNNING PROCESS GOT TO are different things, authored by\ndifferent parties, and a consumer asking for the work plan must not be handed\nBPMN instance state. **That reasoning still holds. The mechanism was the\nmistake.**"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-11",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The dot-prefix guard tests every segment, not just the first",
      "prov:value": "**Why invisibility is worth a guard at all:** a dot-prefixed directory is absent\nfrom a plain `ls`, from most file browsers, and from a forge's web tree. When\nthe work plan and the running-process state lived behind dots, the two artefacts\na person looks for first were the two hardest to find."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-12",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "The conventional layout",
      "prov:value": "### Where a NEW instance puts its code: `<stub>/src` (bean `ylj7`)",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean ylj7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-13",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "A sub-sub-graph — one RESERVED name inside a declared directory",
      "prov:value": "Owner, 2026-09-22, on where a vendor-specific override of a voice belongs:",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-22"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-22"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-14",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A sub-sub-graph — one RESERVED name inside a declared directory",
      "prov:value": "**Adding the name to the convention is not enough; the READER has to descend.**\n`voiceFilesIn` scanned one level and took a directory for a voice only where it\nheld a `voice.json`. `vendors/` holds none — its children do — so every\noverride under it would have been skipped in silence and `loadVoices` would\nhave reported a clean read over real content. That is `dh4f` exactly. It was\nfound by reading the reader when the layout was chosen rather than after\nshipping into it, and `voices-viz.test.ts` asserts both directions: a voice\nunder `vendors/` loads, a voice under any other nested directory does not."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-15",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Assets sit at `<stub>/<asset>` — no declaration reaches down (ENFORCED)",
      "prov:value": "The owner, 2026-09-22: *\"dont bury sub-graph assets. same for `<stub>/skills`,\netc.\"* and *\"follow established norms on layout. qa to enforce.\"*",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-22"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-22"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-16",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Assets sit at `<stub>/<asset>` — no declaration reaches down (ENFORCED)",
      "prov:value": "> **Read with §\"Nesting is declared FROM WITHIN\" below, which is the newer\n> ruling and the one that governs.** This section was titled *\"nothing nests\"*\n> until the two were merged, and that was too absolute: nesting is permitted,\n> **described by a node in the first subdirectory**. What is forbidden either\n> way — and what `check:layout-norms` actually measures — is a ROOT\n> DECLARATION enumerating a path it cannot verify, which is one of the two\n> things #980 rules out explicitly.\n>\n> So the two agree on every case in the corpus today, and the relocation this\n> section describes is *more* correct under the newer ruling than under the\n> one it was made for: `methodology-crdm` at `methodologies/crdm/` was a root\n> declaration reaching down a multi-level path, and removing it rather than\n> repointing it is what #980 requires.\n>\n> **The check will need to learn the from-within node when it exists.** Its\n> kind and name are open and are the owner's (#980), so nothing declares\n> nesting from within today and every pair the check finds is still a root\n> declaration reaching down. The day that node lands, a nesting it describes\n> is legitimate and `check:layout-norms` must stop reporting it — recorded\n> here so the next session reads the constraint instead of filing a false\n> finding against a sanctioned structure.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#980"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-17",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Assets sit at `<stub>/<asset>` — no declaration reaches down (ENFORCED)",
      "prov:value": "**Depth is not the test, and reaching for it produces false findings\nimmediately.** the IRIS replica's `library/` is two segments from the repository root and\nis exactly right. `test/results/` is two segments from its instance root and is\nalso right, because `test/` is not a graph. What is wrong is *containment*: a\ndeclared directory inside another declared directory, which forces a consumer\nscanning the outer one to decide whether the inner one's nodes are also its own\n— bean `x4v4`'s question, and every count computed from that sweep depends on\nthe answer.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean x4v4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-18",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The part that is easy to get wrong",
      "prov:value": "That is not a judgement call made in prose: on 2026-09-22 CRDM's skills were\nrepointed from `methodologies/crdm/` to `skills/process/crdm/` with the entry kept, and\n`gen-skill-docs` immediately demanded a category under **both** the basename\nand the declaration id, because both discovery branches found the one\ndirectory. The three entries were dropped and `methodologies/` now holds its\nfour nodes and no subgraph.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-22"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-22"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-19",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "How it is enforced",
      "prov:value": "Removing a nesting prints as `FIXED` and never fails: a guard that punished the\nfix it exists to encourage is the inversion bean `rl3h` produced elsewhere.\n`--update` shrinks the baseline, so the diff a reviewer sees is the progress.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean rl3h"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-20",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "A subgraph is declared ONCE; every instance that has it is a MEMBER",
      "prov:value": "Owner, 2026-09-30 (option A):",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-21",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A subgraph is declared ONCE; every instance that has it is a MEMBER",
      "prov:value": "Before the per-entry field existed, a fresh folio depending on `cat-harness`\nresolved **12** directories and created all 12 — six of them the platform's\nown, each with a committed keep marker: `dh4f` shipped downstream.\n`perInstance` keeps that fixed."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-22",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The checkout aggregates — no instance declares another's directory (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "Owner, 2026-09-30 (bean `cmsl`, round 3, option A), built by placement PR0\n(bean `ejye`). Until then `cat-harness.json` carried 19 `scope: \"repository\"`\nentries naming its **dependents'** directories (the content core's `skills/`,\nthe IRIS replica's `library/`, …) so that a corpus-wide tool asking the platform for\n\"every library\" got every library. Every owner `needs` cat-harness, so the\nplatform was naming its users — and 5 of the 19 had already drifted from the\nowner's own id.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean cmsl",
        "bean ejye",
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-23",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Nesting is declared FROM WITHIN, by a node in the first subdirectory (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "Owner's ruling, 2026-09-22:",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-22"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-22"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-24",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Nesting is declared FROM WITHIN, by a node in the first subdirectory (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "### A from-within entry can be an INSTANCE directory — bean `cmsl`, 2026-09-30",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean cmsl",
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-25",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A from-within entry can be an INSTANCE directory — bean `cmsl`, 2026-09-30",
      "prov:value": "A kind's `declarationFile` names the node. What an entry in it IS depends on\none field: **an entry marked `\"subgraph\": true` is an instance directory**\n(until 2026-09-30 the marker was answering the now-retired `dependents`),\nand `resolveDirectories` lists it exactly as if `<instance>.json` had declared\nit. It carries `within` (the id of the directory that declared it), and\n`check:layout-norms` treats that pair as the sanctioned shape. An entry\nwithout the marker (`beans.json`'s `defs`, `docs.json`'s `proposals`,\n`voices.json`'s `vendors`) is a part of one graph and stays out of the\ninstance list, exactly as before.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-26",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A from-within entry can be an INSTANCE directory — bean `cmsl`, 2026-09-30",
      "prov:value": "Three rules the resolver holds, all measured the hard way:"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-27",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Concern groups — a sub-subgraph in every grouping kind (placement PR0c)",
      "prov:value": "Owner, 2026-09-29/30 (bean `9umr`): eight groups — `sdlc`, `process`,\n`tools`, `kg`, `library`, `content`, `ui`, `conduct` — *\"built into\nlocation\"*, split across *schemas, skills, uml, processes, library, tests*.\nThe same from-within rule, one more use of it:",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 9umr",
        "2026-09-29"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-29"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-28",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Concern groups — a sub-subgraph in every grouping kind (placement PR0c)",
      "prov:value": "### The live case this ruling settles"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-29",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The live case this ruling settles",
      "prov:value": "**Moved 2026-10-09** (owner: *\"move content/docs into docs/\"*). The source now\nlives at `docs/source/`, inside the docs graph it renders, and is declared FROM\nWITHIN by the docs graph's own node, `docs/docs.json` (entry `docs-source`) —\nthe ruled half above, applied. Readers ask `docsSourceDirFor(root)` rather than\nspelling the path. **The kind is ruled too:** that entry carries the existing\n`docs` kind (owner, 2026-10-09: *\"docs kind is fine\"*), so the source node is\nan existing kind used positionally rather than a new one.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-09"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-30",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The live case this ruling settles",
      "prov:value": "### What the owner left open, and it is NOT an agent's to settle"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-31",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Making a field REQUIRED is a change other branches pay for",
      "prov:value": "The now-retired `dependents` field had no default, deliberately: `reproduce`\nshipped junk downstream and `skip` silently denied a folio its ingestion queue,\nso a field whose wrong value is invisible either way had to be written down.\nThat reasoning was sound and the **cost landed somewhere else**, which is the\npart worth knowing before you do it again. (It was retired 2026-09-30 for a\ndifferent reason — see §\"A subgraph is declared ONCE\" above.)",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-32",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Making a field REQUIRED is a change other branches pay for",
      "prov:value": "Measured 2026-09-20, within an hour of the change: `main` added\n`methodology-crdm` and `methodology-raci` while the field was in review. Neither\ncarried it — that branch's author had no reason to know it existed. On the\nmerged tree **every** `readDeclaration` threw, and CI reported **166 failures\nand 35 errors** whose only visible cause was a raw Zod dump repeated across\nevery test that reads a declaration.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-33",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where a subgraph gets its content — `source` (owner, 2026-10-03)",
      "prov:value": "> *\"It's the same pattern. One of them is mounting the directory while one of\n> them is mounting a branch. Another mount type could come in the future. A sub\n> graph declares where it's getting its content.. It could also be a graph\n> database in the future. That same information can be overwritten by the\n> harness instance config.\"*"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-34",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where a subgraph gets its content — `source` (owner, 2026-10-03)",
      "prov:value": "- **`source`** on a `directories[]` entry is a discriminated union on `kind`.\n  `{ \"kind\": \"directory\" }` — the default, so absent means it — says the\n  content IS the checkout at `path`. `{ \"kind\": \"branch\", \"branch\":\n  \"cat/cat-harness/todos\", \"keyedBy\": \"tip\" }` says the content lives on a\n  declared repository branch and `path` is where a mount of it lands. A later\n  kind (a graph database) is a new member of the union: additive, and a\n  compile error at every consumer that has not decided what to do with it.\n- **The branch NAME is declared once**, on the directory entry that owns it:\n  its `storage` (or `source`). There is no central table — `special-branches.json`\n  was removed on 2026-10-05 (owner: *\"dont use /get rid of\"*). A script that\n  resolves a family's branch reads the folio's declaration first and keeps\n  its built-in names only as a fallback (`cache-family-fallbacks.test.ts`\n  keeps those in step). A branch no declaration names is a finding\n  (`subgraph-source.test.ts`), never a guess at what to fetch.\n- **The instance config overrides it, by id.** `\"subgraphSources\":\n  { \"<dir-id>\": <source> }` inline on the instance's `index.config.json`\n  entry ([`index-config`](index-config.md); a legacy `<instance>.config.json`\n  is read only until it is retired, bean `ymvt`).\n  `subgraphSources` moves a declared directory's content. Bringing in a whole\n  instance from another repository is the entry's `source.remote`, which is a\n  different field. The declaration says what the\n  subgraph IS; the config says how THIS instantiation is set up, and where\n  content is mounted from is that kind of fact. Matched on `id`, never `path`\n  — §\"Inheritance\". The checkout root's config has the last word.\n- **One resolver, and every consumer asks it**: `declaredSubgraph(start, id)`\n  in `schemas/kg/harness-config.ts`, through `resolveSubgraphSource`. Precedence:\n  config override → `source` → a legacy `storage` → `directory`. The answer\n  carries `declaredIn`, so an override is never silent. Never read `source`\n  off the declaration yourself — that skips the override.\n- **`storage` (arc `3fva`, #1764/#1937) maps exactly**: `storage: { branch,\n  keyedBy }` is `source: { kind: \"branch\", branch, keyedBy }`. The resolver\n  reads either and refuses an entry carrying both — two answers to one\n  question. A `qa` subgraph keyed by `tip` is refused whichever field says it.\n  `storage` is the LEGACY spelling: write `source` in a new entry. The\n  presence checks (`materialiseDirectories`, `check:declared-dirs`,\n  `audit:coverage`) ask `contentIsOffCheckout`, which honours both.\n- **Mounting dispatches on the kind**, and the process is\n  `processes/kg/mount-subgraph.bpmn`: `directory` is the checkout path in place\n  (a write is a commit); `branch` + `tip` is mounted from the tip and spliced\n  back without force (`branch-store mount`/`push`); `remote` is another\n  repository's tree at a full 40-character pin, laid down and locked by\n  `mount:remote` and never written back (the [`remote-mount`](remote-mount.md)\n  skill, bean `0mpw`); a kind with no flow is refused with its own exit code\n  rather than read as an empty directory. A remote mount's effective path must\n  not collide with downstream declared directories, reserved root/route names\n  (`RESERVED_ROOT_AND_ROUTE_NAMES`), or sibling mounts (`checkMountPathCollisions`,\n  bean `t4xb`, [`remote-mount`](remote-mount.md)).\n- **The KG export publishes the resolved source** on the Subgraph node, as\n  `contentSource` — `dcterms:source`, with `kind` (`dcterms:type`), `branch`\n  (`dcterms:identifier`), `keyedBy` and `declaredIn` scoped inside it. A\n  GitHub-hosted branch gets an `@id` that is its tree URL.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean ymvt",
        "bean 0mpw",
        "bean t4xb",
        "bean 3fva",
        "#1764",
        "2026-10-05"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-05"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-35",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where a NEW instance's state lives — on its own branch, by default (owner, 2026-10-06)",
      "prov:value": "Measured 2026-10-06: a folio scaffolded by `folio_init` carried `beans/` and\n`todos/` on `main` from its install commit, and its declaration named neither,\nso nothing could say where its work plan lived. The scaffold never asked the\n`source` resolver. The owner's ruling: *fix the process*. Bean `hp54`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean hp54",
        "2026-10-06"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-36",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where a NEW instance's state lives — on its own branch, by default (owner, 2026-10-06)",
      "prov:value": "- **The default is a property of the KIND, read once at scaffold time.** A\n  graph typology may carry `newInstanceSource: { kind: \"branch\", keyedBy:\n  \"tip\" }`, beside `perInstance`. `beans`, `todos` and `fsh-guts` carry it\n  (`fsh-guts` since 2026-10-06, so a new instance's first cutover has\n  somewhere to deposit — see the main half below). It is a storage fact and\n  says nothing about `holds`: `fsh-guts` stays `context`. It has no\n  `branch`, because a kind has no branch name to give. `folio_init` composes\n  the name and writes a **complete** `source` into the new instance's own\n  `<instance>.json`. After that the declaration is the one answer. The\n  resolver never reads `newInstanceSource`, so it is not a second place\n  answering where the content lives. It also cannot move an existing\n  instance whose entry declares no source. This repository's own `beans/`\n  and `todos/` stay where they are until their cutover (`fs43`).\n- **The branch name is `cat/<instance>/<directory-id>`**, built by\n  `instanceStateBranch` in `schemas/kg/subgraph-source.ts`. For example, the\n  `dpi-h-ra` instance's branches are `cat/dpi-h-ra/beans` and\n  `cat/dpi-h-ra/todos`. This is the owner's\n  `cat/<harness>/<name>` ruling (2026-10-02) applied to a folio. One name does\n  three jobs:\n  - it names the declaration (`<instance>.json`);\n  - it prefixes the bean ids (`.beans.yml` → `<instance>-`);\n  - it names the state branches.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean fs43",
        "2026-10-06",
        "2026-10-02"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-02",
        "2026-10-06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-37",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where a NEW instance's state lives — on its own branch, by default (owner, 2026-10-06)",
      "prov:value": "  | resolved | what `folio_init` does |\n  |---|---|\n  | `branch` | Declares the graph. Does **not** write it into the checkout. Adds `/<path>/**` to `.gitignore` so a mount is never committed. Prints the seed command. |\n  | `directory` | Writes the graph **and** declares it. Before `hp54` the scaffold wrote it and did not declare it. |"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-38",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where a NEW instance's state lives — on its own branch, by default (owner, 2026-10-06)",
      "prov:value": "  Then `state:mount` puts each branch at its declared path. This was checked\n  end to end on a fresh repository with a bare remote: init, seed, mount,\n  `beans create`, `branch-store push --id beans`, `beans:claim`.\n- **Cutting an EXISTING instance over is two halves, and both have a command.**\n  Run both from the instance's own repository. Every default is the cwd's git\n  toplevel, and `--repo-root <dir>` overrides it. Until `hp54` the default\n  was the platform checkout, so a folio that links the platform refreshed\n  the platform's branch."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-39",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where a NEW instance's state lives — on its own branch, by default (owner, 2026-10-06)",
      "prov:value": "  **What a cutover removes goes to `fsh-guts` first — the one rule for it**\n  (owner, 2026-10-06: *\"cutover dirs should go to fsh-guts\"*). Before any\n  `git rm`, `--commit` packs `<path>/` at `HEAD` with `git archive`, checks\n  the pack extracts to **the same tree id** it is about to remove, and\n  splices the existing `retired/` pair onto the trashcan's tip through\n  `branch-store` (never a force push, `expect: null` so it never\n  overwrites): `retired/cutover-<instance>-<dir>-<tree12>.tar.gz` plus a\n  same-basename `.md` declaring `folio-fsh-guts/v1`, `kind:\n  cutover-snapshot`, `movedFrom`, `movedOn`, `reason: cutover`, the\n  instance, `sourceCommit`, `tree` and `authoritativeBranch`. It re-reads\n  the tip, and only if both blobs are there does it make the removal\n  commit. No trashcan declared, a trashcan not on a tip, a branch that is\n  not there, or a push that did not land: **refused, `main` untouched**,\n  with the fix named. This is not a second disposal of beans — the branch\n  is still the live store; the deposit is the copy `main` last held.\n  `deletion-requires-confirmation` still governs: the dry run is the\n  report, and `--commit` is only run on the owner's explicit go.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-06"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-40",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "What a derived subgraph is computed FROM — `derivedFrom` (owner, 2026-10-04)",
      "prov:value": "The owner's three rulings (bean `nama`; design note\n`docs/proposals/derived-graph-dependencies-2026-10-04.md`):",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean nama",
        "2026-10-04",
        "docs/proposals/derived-graph-dependencies-2026-10-04.md"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-41",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "proposal",
      "cat-harness:section": "What a derived subgraph is computed FROM — `derivedFrom` (owner, 2026-10-04)",
      "prov:value": "1. **The edge sits on the DERIVED side.** The writer of a derived graph is what knows its\n   inputs, so the edge points down the stack: an IG's pages name its artefact index, and\n   the index never lists the pages. A source-side `rendersTo` (proposal #1966) was\n   rejected because upstream graphs would have to name graphs above them.\n2. **Generated pages and ingested indexes are in the `derived` layer**, as `library/` is,\n   so a QA finding against them goes to their generator.\n3. **An edge across a missing `needs` path is a RATCHET, not an error.** Example: an IG's\n   pages are styled by the DAK layer's chrome, but the IG does not `need` the DAK layer. The\n   gate holds such an edge as a baselined layering gap with a reason. The dependency\n   stays declared and the gap stays visible.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#1966"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-42",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "What a derived subgraph is computed FROM — `derivedFrom` (owner, 2026-10-04)",
      "prov:value": "**Data graphs only.** A generator is code. The staging cone (bean `4j86`) reaches it\nthrough its import closure, so code is not declared a second time here.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 4j86"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-43",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The storage clock — a derived artefact is kept no closer to main than its inputs (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "Owner, 2026-10-05, after one `state:push` turned every open PR red: *\"how to\nfix process so wont fail?\"* and *\"use dependencies between KG derivations and\nsuch (caches, auto-docs, etc)\"*. Bean `0b8c`, #2230.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 0b8c",
        "#2230",
        "2026-10-05"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-05"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-44",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The storage clock — a derived artefact is kept no closer to main than its inputs (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "**The measured failure.** The fsh-guts viewer was committed on main while\n`fsh-guts/` is kept on `cat/cat-harness/fsh-guts`. On 2026-10-05 a `state:push`\n(bean `rva2`) relocated files there, and `fsh-guts:viz:check` went red on main\nand on every open PR at once, until someone regenerated and committed the page —\nwhich a sibling then had to repeat on its own PR. Bean `foaq` was the same\ndesign seen from a dirty local mount. A take-base merge pattern for the page\n(it had one) only made the collision cheap; this removes it.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean rva2",
        "bean foaq",
        "2026-10-05"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-05"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-45",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Publishing a subgraph's contents — the declared Subgraph node is the container (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "Owner, 2026-10-03: *\"todos = subgraph node + todo content nodes\"*. A generator\nthat publishes the contents of a declared subgraph (a `todos.jsonld`, a beans\ngraph, a library index) does NOT mint its own collection node for them. It:",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-03"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-03"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-46",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Publishing a subgraph's contents — the declared Subgraph node is the container (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "One trap, measured while building it: do not restate the subgraph's `name` on\na container written to `<dir>/<id>.json`. A file whose `name` equals its stem\nis what `findDeclarationFile` reads as an INSTANCE declaration, and the site\ndirectory became an instance root that hid the checkout from every resolver."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-47",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Publishing a subgraph's contents — the declared Subgraph node is the container (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "### A consumer never spells a declared directory's path (bean `gz47`, ENFORCED)",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean gz47"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-48",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A consumer never spells a declared directory's path (bean `gz47`, ENFORCED)",
      "prov:value": "| gate | what it scans | baseline |\n|---|---|---|\n| `check:declared-paths` | an instance's literals into its OWN declared directories | `cat-harness-tools/scripts/declared-path-baseline.json` |\n| `check:foreign-paths` | an instance's literals into ANOTHER instance's directories, across the whole checkout | `cat-harness-tools/scripts/foreign-path-baseline.json` — **empty since 2026-10-03** |",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-03"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-03"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-49",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "A consumer never spells a declared directory's path (bean `gz47`, ENFORCED)",
      "prov:value": "- **Classify a hit by what it READS, not by how it is spelled.** A name counts\n  as foreign only if the scanning instance does not declare the same name. A\n  `docs/` joined onto a STAGED instance's root, or matched inside that\n  instance's own declaration, belongs to that instance even though the name\n  coincides.\n- **A site that cannot read a declaration says why**, on the line above:\n  `// declared-path-literal: <reason>`. The reason is required. A marked site\n  is still reported, just not counted. Legitimate cases: the convention\n  fallback for an instance that declares nothing, an ignore file, and a path\n  inside another instance's declaration.\n- **A file that exists today is not a stable path** (owner ruling 2026-10-04).\n  `check:declared-paths` used to accept any literal naming an existing file,\n  and only fired once that file moved. Under the content-source ruling the\n  move is the expected case, so the exemption is now a one-way baseline. The\n  witnesses recorded on 2026-10-04 stay. A NEW literal naming an existing file\n  fails the gate. `--update` may drop a witness but refuses to add one, so the\n  list only shrinks. Four such literals had landed unnoticed before the\n  baseline was frozen.\n- **The known blind spot:** only the first literal of a path call is read, so\n  `resolve(opt(\"todos\") ?? \"todos\")` hides its default. Review such a default\n  by hand.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-04"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-50",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Declare what exists",
      "prov:value": "This repository's own declaration is the worked example, and **no count is\ngiven here on purpose**: `<name>.json` is the list. That sentence said\n\"declares `schemas/` and `skills/`\" until 2026-09-20, by which point it\ndeclared **twenty-one** directories — a number in prose is a claim, and this\none had been false for long enough that `AGENTS.md` carries its own flagged\ncopy of the same rot.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-51",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "`storage` — a directory kept on a branch",
      "prov:value": "A `ContentDirectory` may declare where its contents are KEPT when that is not\nthe checkout (bean `16ei`, arc `3fva`, and proposal `state-branch-2026-10-02`;\nschema `DirectoryStorageSchema` in `schemas/kg/cat-harness.ts`, or its modern\n`source: { kind: \"branch\", ... }` spelling in `schemas/kg/subgraph-source.ts`):",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 16ei",
        "bean 3fva",
        "2026-10-02"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-52",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "`storage` — a directory kept on a branch",
      "prov:value": "### A branch's README is rendered, not written (bean `esz4`)",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean esz4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-53",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "A branch's README is rendered, not written (bean `esz4`)",
      "prov:value": "- the **declarations** — every `directories[]` entry whose `storage` or branch\n  `source` names the branch, read where each instance declares it. Never a\n  table of branches: owner, 2026-10-03 (`rva2`), *\"each harness declares it,\n  (and each instance can also declare), why centralize?\"*;\n- the branch's root **`manifest.json`**, `state-manifest/v1`, whose field set\n  is `schemas/kg/state-manifest.ts`: one shape for a tip-keyed branch (seed\n  commit, `graphs`) and one for a route-keyed branch (`writer`, `gate`).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean rva2",
        "2026-10-03"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-03"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-54",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Authoring an entry — the checklist",
      "prov:value": "1. **`id`, `path`, `graphTypologies`** — required. Refer to the entry by `id`\n   everywhere else; **ids are stable, paths are not** (§\"Declare what exists\",\n   §\"Inheritance\"). Pick each kind by what a process does with it\n   ([`content-context-and-state-graphs`](content-context-and-state-graphs.md)).\n2. **`title`** — a short noun phrase a reader would put on a tab. It is the\n   directory README's heading; absent, the heading falls back to the id and\n   `subgraph-readmes` records a `no-title` finding.\n3. **`description`** — one or two sentences **for a reader**: what the\n   directory holds and what it is for, **60 words at most**\n   (`DESCRIPTION_WORDS`, finding `long-description`). It is printed under the\n   README heading. History, rulings, measurements and argument go in a\n   `_description_comment` on the same entry (the `_<field>_comment` convention\n   the entries already use) — `_` keys are annotations every loader drops\n   (§\"Node schemas, one per `$schema` family\").\n4. **Declare only what exists**, or say why not with `absent: { reason }`.\n   **Where its content comes from** is `source` — omit it for the checkout\n   directory; a branch source names its branch on the entry itself\n   (§\"Where a subgraph gets its content\").\n5. **`coverage`** — the `skill` that governs it and the `docs` that say what it\n   is for; an opt-out carries its reason (`SubgraphCoverageSchema`). Without a\n   skill the directory is unreachable by an agent even where a person can read\n   it. **The visualiser is NOT declared here**: the harness declares it, at its\n   own level, in `visualisers` — `covers` this directory's id (or\n   `coversKinds` its kind), `renderedBy` a Tool — and it is published at\n   `<base>/<harness>/<visualiser>/[<sub-graph>/]`, computed by\n   `visualiserRoute` (owner, 2026-10-09; `harness-tiles` §\"Where a tile goes\").\n   A directory never names its page, and a page never claims a directory; two\n   declarations on one route fail `check:visualiser-routes`. The visualiser is\n   where a node's RENDERING IRIs are derived from; the node never names them —\n   `kg-viewer` §\"The asset and its rendering are two resources with two IRIs\".\n6. **The files inside declare what they are** — front matter, a `$schema`, a\n   leading comment — never an extension or a location\n   (§\"A sub-sub-graph\", §\"Every other marker\"). The README's \"what it is\"\n   column reads exactly that, so a file that says nothing shows as a gap.\n7. **Generated or authored — say which, in the file.** A generated file\n   carries the markers of the region it owns\n   ([`readme-sections`](../../ui/ui-core/readme-sections.md)) or a header naming the command\n   that wrote it, is never hand-edited, and has a `--check` twin in CI.\n8. **A missing fact is a QA finding, never a blank or a guess** — the three\n   states of §\"Three states, as everywhere else here\". Record it as a QA\n   result under `test/results/` (the working copy `qa-publish` stores on the\n   `qa-reports` branch); do not fill it with a plausible default.\n9. **Then regenerate**: `bun run cat readme:subgraphs` renders the directory's\n   README from this entry ([`liquid-templates`](../../ui/ui-core/liquid-templates.md)), and\n   `bun run cat kg:export` publishes the entry as a Directory node\n   ([`kg-export`](kg-export.md)).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-09"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-55",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Naming — a self-identifying declaration, stub-named artefacts (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "Modelled on WHO's SMART Base repository's `dak.json` (ours is the `dak`\nsection of `index.config.json`'s `contentTypes` since 2026-10-24; upstream's\nspelling is unchanged), which carries\n`canonicalUrl`, `publicationUrl` and `previewUrl` and derives its stub by\nstripping the repository's prefix (`smart-<stub>` → `<stub>` →\n`https://smart.who.int/<stub>`), so stub, directory and published path are one\nword.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-24"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-24"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-56",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Identity: `repository` and `livesAt` (owner, 2026-09-30, bean `6rmv`)",
      "prov:value": "It is **planned** rather than current because IRIs are keyed by it and must\nsurvive the split. The pre-split fact lives in `livesAt`: `cat-harness` is\nthe `cat-harness/` directory of `litlfred/folio-assistant`. Leaving `livesAt`\nout asserts that the instance already sits at its own repository's root."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-57",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Identity: `repository` and `livesAt` (owner, 2026-09-30, bean `6rmv`)",
      "prov:value": "Since 2026-09-30 (bean `yn2k`, the owner's decision), `cat-harness` and\nthe content core declare an `iriBase` on their planned repositories'\nhosts, versioned as bootstrap's is:\n- `https://litlfred.github.io/cat-harness/v0/ns/`\n- `https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-core/v0/ns/`",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean yn2k",
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-58",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Every other marker: THE TYPE DECLARES ITS OWN FILENAME (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "Settled by the owner 2026-09-20, bean `79t3` question 1. The rule above covers\nthe root declaration and published artefacts. For every **other** marker — the\nfiles by which a repository asserts what it is — there is no uniform spelling\nand there cannot be one:",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 79t3",
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-59",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Every other marker: THE TYPE DECLARES ITS OWN FILENAME (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "| marker | who owns the name |\n|---|---|\n| `sushi-config.yaml` | SUSHI reads that exact name, and it is YAML |\n| `index.config.json` `contentTypes.dak` | **ours** — bean `cz17`. Was `dak.json`; the owner moved it into the index on 2026-10-24 (\"WHO will adopt harness file\"). It is a SECTION, not a file: the content type declares `{ indexSection: \"dak\" }` (`IndexSectionMarker`). WHO's SMART Base still writes and reads `dak.json`; that IG's build reads that file into the index for one build, with a warning, until an IG moves it |\n| `index.config.json` `contentTypes.ig` | **The FHIR layer's**: bean `cz17`, item 3. On 2026-10-30 the owner chose \"A. ig section in index.config\" over treating `sushi-config.yaml` as the marker. A repository declares itself the source of a FHIR IG with this section, registered as `{ indexSection: \"ig\" }` by https://github.com/litlfred/fhir-harness/blob/main/schemas/ig-content-type.ts. Carrying `sushi-config.yaml` alone does not declare an IG. The section's `include` lists the paths the harness reads. See \"A marker declares what is read\", below |\n| `<name>.json`, `<name>.config.json` | ours |\n| `beans.json`, `todos.json` | ours, and named after the graph TYPOLOGY |",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean cz17",
        "2026-10-24",
        "2026-10-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-24",
        "2026-10-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-60",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Every other marker: THE TYPE DECLARES ITS OWN FILENAME (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "One of those is not ours to rename — `sushi-config.yaml`, whose name SUSHI\nowns — so any rule claiming to cover the set would still be false on arrival.\nIt was two until 2026-09-22, when `dak.json` was renamed on the owner's word\n(and on 2026-10-24 became a section of the index);\nthat it *could* be renamed is what bean `cz17` settled, against this table's\nown earlier claim. **So the type declares its filename, and the\nconvention is that rule rather than a spelling.** What a new type copies is\n*say what your marker is called*, not a pattern to imitate.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean cz17",
        "2026-09-22",
        "2026-10-24"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-22",
        "2026-10-24"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-61",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Every other marker: THE TYPE DECLARES ITS OWN FILENAME (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "**At the graph-typology level this is `GraphTypologyDef.declarationFile`**, and it is\nalready load-bearing rather than decorative. `declaredKinds` computed a nested\ndeclaration's filename as `${basename(path)}.json` while `bean-graph.ts` held\nthat *moving `beans/` to `work/` requires editing nothing inside it*. Both were\ntrue of today's layout and contradict each other on the first relocation: the\nwalk looks for `work/work.json`, the file is still `work/beans.json`, and the\nnested kinds drop out of `declared` with nothing said — an under-count, which\nmanufactures an `undeclared` finding somewhere else. The kind now names its own\nfile, `BEAN_GRAPH_FILE` and `TODO_GRAPH_FILE` derive from it, and the\ndirectory-name convention remains only as a fallback for a kind that declares\nnone."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-62",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "A marker declares what is read, and nothing else is (owner, 2026-10-30)",
      "prov:value": "> \"a harness can point to a repo, but doesnt want to automatically slurpo up\n> all content in a repo. harness desclares what it it pays attention to in the\n> repo.\" (owner, 2026-10-30T12:41Z, bean `cz17`)",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean cz17",
        "2026-10-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-63",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "`docs/<stub>/` — the site is packaged for the split (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "**It is not the `folio` graph-typology declaration.** `docs/` still does not appear\nin `<name>.json`: measured 2026-09-19, adding it makes `harness:dirs`,\n`kg:schema:check` and `docs:harness:check` throw `unknown graph typology \"folio\"`,\nbecause `folio` is contributed by **core** and those readers do not load its\nregistration. Packaging is unblocked; the declaration waits. Bean `x4a6`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean x4a6",
        "2026-09-19"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-19"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-64",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "`docs/<stub>/` — the site is packaged for the split (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "**`scripts/tests/site-dir-single-answer.test.ts` fails on any new `docs/…`\nliteral in a path-resolving position** (prose is left alone). It found 154\nstale paths on its first run, including the e2e suite that `bun test` never\nruns, and five more that arrived later in a clean merge from a branch cut\nbefore the move — which is the case it exists for."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-65",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Layering — a harness module must not import the content vocabulary",
      "prov:value": "The declaration schema needs the platform's IRI namespace to mint `@type`\nvalues. That namespace used to live in `schemas/kg/jsonld.ts`, which is\nthe content core's **content** vocabulary — block kinds, DoCO structural\ntypes, SPAR citation terms. Importing it would have made `agentic-harness`\ndepend on the content model for its own type IRIs: a `harness → core` edge,\nalready the largest wrong-direction group `bun run cat check:partition` reports."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-66",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Why Zod — the grounds, and what kind of ground each is",
      "prov:value": "The owner, 2026-09-23: *\"not preference on .ts, fixed on\nfunctionality/pragmatism\"*. The choice is a JUDGEMENT on what the tool does,\nand stating its grounds is what lets a later reader tell whether they still\nhold. **Three are functional and checkable:**",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-23"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-67",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Why not author the rendering and validate backwards",
      "prov:value": "The tempting inversion is to hand-author the JSON-LD node, because it is the\nform the KG consumes, and validate it against Zod in CI. That is the same two\nartefacts with the authority pointing the wrong way, and it is worse for one\nreason: **a hand-authored node is unchecked until CI runs.** With Zod\nauthoritative the schema *is* the type, so a malformed definition fails at\n`tsc`, in the editor, before it is committed. Validation-after-the-fact catches\nthe same error strictly later and only if CI is green — and bean `dzl3` is this\nrepository's evidence that a suite can sit non-running for as long as it has\nexisted.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean dzl3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-68",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Pinning a reference — a SHA may stage, only a version may publish",
      "prov:value": "Owner, 2026-09-20, on how an instance's dependencies are pinned:",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-69",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "A REFERENCE is not PROVENANCE",
      "prov:value": "`bun run cat check:published-refs` enforces this; advisory, `--strict` to fail. It\nreports each carrier's count **even when that count is nought**, because a\ngate that silently covers nothing and exits 0 is this repository's most\nexpensive recurring defect (`xom7`, `dh4f`, `a6kl`).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean xom7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-70",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "proposal",
      "cat-harness:section": "A REFERENCE is not PROVENANCE",
      "prov:value": "Full scheme, including what an instance's version means and what makes it go\nup: [`cat-harness/docs/proposals/instance-versioning.md`](../../../docs/proposals/instance-versioning.md).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "docs/proposals/instance-versioning.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-71",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "`storage` — a directory kept on a branch",
      "prov:value": "Two facts to hold while the arc is in flight. **Every `qa` directory declares\n`storage` since bean `5hox`**, and only after every reader had migrated —\nflipping one earlier would have told the presence checks to stop looking while\nreaders still read the checkout. Each stored working copy is ignored by\nversion control, one line per declared directory, kept equal to the\ndeclarations by `directory-storage.test.ts`. And the derived files are\n**committed on `main`** until bean `5hox`'s removal lands; until then, and\nafter, a directory whose every kind is one the arc moves off `main`\n(`qa`, `health` — `mayLeaveMain` in `scripts/qa-results.ts`) may be absent\nfrom a checkout without that being a finding. **Judgements never move**: they\nare the `attestations` kind at `test/attestations/`, on `main`, because a\nregenerated file cannot reproduce them (owner ruling D2 (a)).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean 5hox"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-72",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where a NEW instance puts its code: `<stub>/src` (bean `ylj7`)",
      "prov:value": "**Being declared is not being reached.** Those are two questions and\n`bun run cat check:code-accounting` reports them apart — never averaged, because a\nrise in one can hide a fall in the other. Beans `d308` and `ce65` own the\nsecond.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean ce65"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-73",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where a NEW instance puts its code: `<stub>/src` (bean `ylj7`)",
      "prov:value": "**Being declared is not being reached.** Those are two questions and\n`bun run cat check:code-accounting` reports them apart — never averaged, because a\nrise in one can hide a fall in the other. Beans `d308` and `ce65` own the\nsecond.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean d308"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-74",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "The live case this ruling settles",
      "prov:value": "Bean `hs08` had recorded the absent `content` entry as the rename having\n*landed*. It is the opposite: the declaration was emptied of `content/` **before**\nthe directory holding the content moved, leaving 14 live subgraphs invisible to\nany consumer that scans the declaration. That is `dh4f` pointed the other way —\n**present-but-undeclared** rather than declared-but-absent, and the worse of the\ntwo, because the clean run is over content that is really there.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean hs08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md#provenance-75",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where a subgraph gets its content — `source` (owner, 2026-10-03)",
      "prov:value": "A \"directory subgraph\" and a \"branch subgraph\" are **one thing with two\nsources**, so there is one field and one resolver, never a branch concept\nbeside a directory concept. Bean `l4ay`; schema `schemas/kg/subgraph-source.ts`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean l4ay"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
